    "body": "@bzbarsky \r\n> It's \"fully active\", not \"ready\". And you should follow the link to the definition of \"fully active\"\r\n\r\nI wouldn't have asked the question if I knew this step well. I know the difference between loaded and fully active, I was reading what means `fully active`. Sure I know that browser may save the document objects in a memory for restoration in the future. \r\n\r\n>Yes, when setTimeout is called the document in question might not be fully active, and might become fully active in the future.\r\n>\r\n> >what does not necessarily consecutively (in this context) also mean?\r\n>\r\n> A document is not fully active. setTimeout(1000, func) is called. The document becomes fully active for 300ms, then stops being fully active for 10 minutes. Then it becomes fully active for 500ms, then stops being fully active for 20 minutes. Then it becomes fully active, and after 200ms the timer fires.\r\n\r\nIf I understand you correctly, we can conditionally invoke `setTimeout` on the page `example.com` that will be cached. After that we don't wait timeout (let it will be 5000ms) of the `setTimeout`, then go to the another page, but if we want to go back we can use navigation back. And if we do this, we get performing callback of the `setTimeout` if timeout has finished, otherwise we wait finishing timeout and after that perform callback. Do I correctly understand your thought?\r\n\r\nAnd one thing: Is it possible invoke `setTimeout` on the not `fully active` document?\r\n\r\nBy the way, you have well described `not necessarily consecutively`. Much appreciated for this.",
